is transformation improves the efficiency of the query by reducing the number of rows that need to be joined."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The query rewrite process can be explained as follows:\n\nThe query rewrite rule has applied to the given SQL query by simplifying the constant arithmetic expression `case mean when 0 then null else stdev/mean end` to a literal value. This simplification is done according to Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, which identifies constant expressions in the SELECT list and evaluates them to replace them with literal values.\n\nAdditionally, the CAST operation on the column `d_moy` has been removed, and the original data type has been preserved. This removal is done according to Case 2 of the query rewrite rule, which removes redundant CAST operations that do not alter the data type of the column except to change or adjust nullability.\n\nThe rewritten SQL query retains its original structure and semantics, with the refined expressions in the SELECT list replacing the original expressions. The other aspects of the query (such as WHERE, GROUP BY, ORDER BY clauses) remain unchanged."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""According to Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, the conditions are met when the SQL query contains a `GROUP BY` clause that groups by simple columns (no expressions in the `GROUP BY` list), the aggregate functions in the `SELECT` clause operate directly on columns (no expressions inside the aggregate functions), and the `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nIn this case, the original query meets these conditions. The `GROUP BY` clause groups by simple columns (`w_warehouse_name`, `w_warehouse_sk`, `i_item_sk`, and `d_moy`). The aggregate functions in the `SELECT` clause operate directly on columns (`stdev` and `mean`). The `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nTherefore, the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by transforming it into a new query with different join conditions and aggregate functions."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE clause based on known constraints. In this case, the condition `d_moy=2` is statically determined as always true, so it can be removed from the WHERE clause. Similarly, the condition `inv2.d_moy=2+1` is simplified to `inv2.d_moy=3`, which is also statically determined as always true.\n\nThe rewritten query simplifies the conditions in the WHERE"""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by applying filters in the WHERE clause before GROUP BY operations to decrease the amount of data to be aggregated. For example, the condition `d_year = 2001` can be moved into the WHERE clause before the GROUP BY operation. Additionally, the subqueries used in the SELECT statement can be modified to include filter conditions, moving complex filters closer to the data retrieval steps and optimizing subquery performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by rearranging the query to perform `GROUP BY` operations at the earliest stage, ideally before executing operations like `JOIN`. For example, the subqueries used in the SELECT statement can be modified to include grouping operations, reducing the dataset size early in the execution process."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by moving repeated function calls or operations outside of loops, if applicable. For example, the `expensive_function` used in the SELECT statement can be calculated once before the loop and stored the result for reuse. Additionally, the use of inline functions in the SELECT or WHERE clause can be replaced with a pre-calculated column if the function is deterministic and the input data does not change frequently."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by combining multiple joins into a single join operation using `CASE` statements to conditionally select different attributes from the table in one pass. For example, the joins used in the SELECT statement can be combined into a single join using a `CASE` statement to select the correct attribute based on specific conditions."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 10:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by simplifying constant expressions upfront, for example, transforming `(a<b AND b=c) AND a=5` into `b>5 AND b=c AND a=5`. Additionally, redundant or tautological conditions can be eliminated, and the use of constant values in range conditions can be optimized."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 11:\n"""The given SQL query can be optimized by moving outer query conditions into the WHERE clause of subqueries when these conditions do not affect aggregations or window functions. For example, the condition `inv1.d_moy = 2` can be moved into the WHERE clause of the subquery."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 12:\n"""It would be faster to pre-join the data and select from a single table than to perform a join."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 13:\n"""Create a temporary table joining the values and then querying the chart with those."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 14:\n"""Use a loop to get the data, but it\'s still slow."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 15:\n"""Join several times with the same table function for different input variables in the same query."""'}], 'response': 'Here is a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten by simplifying constant arithmetic expressions, removing redundant CAST operations, and combining multiple joins into a single join operation. Additionally, filters can be moved into the ON clause of an INNER JOIN to reduce the size of the dataset before joining. The query can also be optimized by rearranging the query to perform GROUP BY operations at the earliest stage, moving repeated function calls or operations outside of loops, and eliminating redundant or tautological conditions. Furthermore, constant expressions can be transformed upfront, and outer query conditions can be moved into the WHERE clause of subqueries when these conditions do not affect aggregations or window functions.', 'time': 1.8285534381866455}
